ontribution rating metrics\n- Confidence scoring systems\n\n## What the Document Does Include\n\nThe guide emphasizes that reviewers should:\n\n- Make clear recommendations (\"Accept or Reject\")\n- State \"one or two key reasons for this choice\"\n- Complete a Code of Ethics report\n- Engage in discussion phases\n\nThe two sample reviews provided demonstrate good review practices but don't reference specific numerical rating scales used in the actual review system.\n\n**Note:** The actual rating scales are likely implemented in the OpenReview submission platform itself, which isn't fully documented in these instructions. You may need to consult the OpenReview interface directly or contact ICLR for the complete rating rubric.","durationMs":4892,"url":"https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2025/ReviewerGuide"}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_016oY3f3r3XxLdZECU9DZxqr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me try to find the ICLR reviewer rating scale from another source. I know from my training data that the ICLR rating scale is typically:\n\nFor ICLR 2025/2026, the overall rating scale is:\n- 10: Top 5% of accepted papers, seminal paper\n- 8: Top 15-20% of accepted papers, strong accept\n- 6: Marginally above acceptance threshold\n- 5: Marginally below acceptance threshold  \n- 3: Clear reject\n- 1: Strong reject\n\nBut it seems the 2026 scale may have changed to {0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10}. Let me search more specifically.\n\n\nI have enough information to work with now. I'll use the standard ICLR rating scale that's been established through 2025, which includes the overall 1-10 scale along with separate 1-4 scales for soundness, presentation, and contribution, plus a 1-5 confidence scale, and I'll note which scale I'm using in the review.\n\nNow I'm setting up the review structure with two specialized reviewers—one focusing on methods and experiments, the other on novelty and related work—and I'll run them through the full roundtable process: independent reviews first, then cross-sharing, converging on key points, and finally synthesizing into a cohesive assessment.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":201,"cache_creation_input_tokens":630,"cache_read_input_tokens":60511,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":630},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"583db7b7-de08-4fba-84bb-24a29bad8915","uuid":"db68b4f2-a30e-49ee-9adb-353e027021aa"}
