{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_016oY3f3r3XxLdZECU9DZxqr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01K3dMPo9TcrEJwkGKtZyDGF","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an ICLR paper review. Your focus is **Novelty and Related Work**. \n\n## Rating Scale (ICLR)\n- Overall: 1-10 (1=strong reject, 3=clear reject, 5=marginally below, 6=marginally above, 8=strong accept, 10=top 5%)\n- Soundness: 1-4 (1=poor, 2=fair, 3=good, 4=excellent)\n- Presentation: 1-4\n- Contribution: 1-4\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"Temporal Differential Item Functioning Reveals 31% Measurement Instability in MMLU: A Psychometric Audit of 5,227 Language Models\"\n\nThe paper applies Mantel-Haenszel Differential Item Functioning (MH-DIF) — a classical psychometric method standardized since 1988 in educational testing — to the METABENCH MMLU response matrix (5,227 models × 12,508 items). It constitutes the largest-scale DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks.\n\n**Four claims**:\n1. **Temporal instability**: 31.3% of MMLU items exhibit large temporal DIF (ETS C) between 2023 and 2024-H1 cohorts.\n2. **Ground truth meta-critique**: Web-overlap contamination labels cannot validate item-level differential detectors (enrichment ≈ 1.0).\n3. **Semi-synthetic validation**: ΔTPR methodology with cross-subject matching (ΔFPR = 0.000). Portable to GSM8K.\n4. **DIF as diagnostic lens**: DIF items carry cross-cohort signal but are the *least* disruptive non-random removal strategy; DIF serves as diagnostic tool, not contamination detector.\n\n**Related Work (§2)**:\nThree subsections:\n1. *Benchmark contamination detection*: ConStat (perturbation), Min-K% (memorization), DVD (generation variance). All validate against web-overlap/n-gram ground truth — which measures global exposure, not differential contamination. Paper positions DIF as orthogonal (Jaccard=0.23 with ConStat).\n2. *Psychometrics in NLP evaluation*: IRT applied for item difficulty, adaptive testing, ability measurement. But IRT-based *DIF analysis* not applied to LLM benchmarks at scale. Paper bridges this gap.\n3. *Benchmark validity and temporal dynamics*: \"Growing Pains\" and anchor-item strategies. DIF complements by identifying *which* anchor items degrade.\n\n**Key methodological contribution**: Cross-subject matching in semi-synthetic injection — computing matching variable on subjects other than the tested one eliminates circular FPR inflation (ΔFPR=0.000 vs +0.186 for within-subject).\n\n**Appendix results** (negative findings transparently reported):\n- ConStat comparison: orthogonal detection (Jaccard=0.23)\n- DVD: negative on MCQ (AUROC=0.61)\n- Ensemble fusion: no significant improvement over DIF standalone\n- RL robustness: alignment-RL confirmed, reasoning-RL underpowered (N=13)\n- Bifactor IRT pilot: partial convergence (32/57), below CFI target\n- Item generation: difficulty calibration below target (ρ=0.58 vs 0.75)\n- Cumulative Bayesian DIF: incremental power gains\n- Graceful degradation: quantitative bounds on replacement\n- Item-Focused Trees: robustness to cohort definition (Jaccard=0.74 with MH)\n\n**References** (8 total in main paper):\n- Hendrycks et al. 2021 (MMLU)\n- Holland & Thayer 1988 (MH-DIF)\n- Mantel & Haenszel 1959 (original MH)\n- ETS 1999 (ETS standards)\n- Li et al. 2024 (task contamination)\n- Sheikhan et al. 2024 (ConStat)\n- Shi et al. 2024 (Min-K%)\n- Sinharay et al. 2006 (Bayesian DIF)\n- Tutz & Berger 2016 (Item-Focused Trees)\n- Sheikhan & Mukherjee 2024 (GSM1K)\n\n## Your Task\n\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Novelty assessment**: How novel is applying MH-DIF to LLM benchmarks? Is it a \"first application of known method to new domain\" or deeper? Is the cross-subject matching insight genuinely new?\n2. **Related work completeness**: Are important references missing? Is the positioning fair and accurate?\n3. **Contribution significance**: Would this paper change how the community thinks about benchmark evaluation? Is the diagnostic lens framing compelling?\n4. **Positioning and framing**: Is the paper positioned correctly relative to contamination detection literature? Is the \"instability ≠ invalidity\" distinction well-argued?\n5. **Audience and venue fit**: Is ICLR the right venue? Would reviewers at ICLR find this interesting?\n\nSpecifically consider these related work gaps:\n- Is there prior work on DIF in NLP/LLM settings that's missing?\n- Are there other psychometric approaches to benchmark validity not cited?\n- Is the contamination detection literature adequately covered (beyond ConStat, Min-K%, DVD)?\n- Are there benchmark dynamics papers (saturation, temporal validity) that should be cited?\n- \"Growing Pains\" is mentioned but not fully cited — identify this paper.\n\nProvide scores for: Overall (1-10), Soundness (1-4), Presentation (1-4), Contribution (1-4), Confidence (1-5).\n\nWrite your review in a structured format with: Summary, Strengths, Weaknesses, Questions for Authors, Missing References, Scores. Be thorough and critical but fair.\n","model":"opus"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":201,"cache_creation_input_tokens":630,"cache_read_input_tokens":60511,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":630},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"583db7b7-de08-4fba-84bb-24a29bad8915","uuid":"89db4d3e-2309-47ef-ae5e-b22402bf1c8d"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a866039ced70ad079","tool_use_id":"toolu_01K3dMPo9TcrEJwkGKtZyDGF","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an ICLR paper review. Your focus is **Novelty and Related Work**. \n\n## Rating Scale (ICLR)\n- Overall: 1-10 (1=strong reject, 3=clear reject, 5=marginally below, 6=marginally above, 8=strong accept, 10=top 5%)\n- Soundness: 1-4 (1=poor, 2=fair, 3=good, 4=excellent)\n- Presentation: 1-4\n- Contribution: 1-4\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"Temporal Differential Item Functioning Reveals 31% Measurement Instability in MMLU: A Psychometric Audit of 5,227 Language Models\"\n\nThe paper applies Mantel-Haenszel Differential Item Functioning (MH-DIF) — a classical psychometric method standardized since 1988 in educational testing — to the METABENCH MMLU response matrix (5,227 models × 12,508 items). It constitutes the largest-scale DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks.\n\n**Four claims**:\n1. **Temporal instability**: 31.3% of MMLU items exhibit large temporal DIF (ETS C) between 2023 and 2024-H1 cohorts.\n2. **Ground truth meta-critique**: Web-overlap contamination labels cannot validate item-level differential detectors (enrichment ≈ 1.0).\n3. **Semi-synthetic validation**: ΔTPR methodology with cross-subject matching (ΔFPR = 0.000). Portable to GSM8K.\n4. **DIF as diagnostic lens**: DIF items carry cross-cohort signal but are the *least* disruptive non-random removal strategy; DIF serves as diagnostic tool, not contamination detector.\n\n**Related Work (§2)**:\nThree subsections:\n1. *Benchmark contamination detection*: ConStat (perturbation), Min-K% (memorization), DVD (generation variance). All validate against web-overlap/n-gram ground truth — which measures global exposure, not differential contamination. Paper positions DIF as orthogonal (Jaccard=0.23 with ConStat).\n2. *Psychometrics in NLP evaluation*: IRT applied for item difficulty, adaptive testing, ability measurement. But IRT-based *DIF analysis* not applied to LLM benchmarks at scale. Paper bridges this gap.\n3. *Benchmark validity and temporal dynamics*: \"Growing Pains\" and anchor-item strategies. DIF complements by identifying *which* anchor items degrade.\n\n**Key methodological contribution**: Cross-subject matching in semi-synthetic injection — computing matching variable on subjects other than the tested one eliminates circular FPR inflation (ΔFPR=0.000 vs +0.186 for within-subject).\n\n**Appendix results** (negative findings transparently reported):\n- ConStat comparison: orthogonal detection (Jaccard=0.23)\n- DVD: negative on MCQ (AUROC=0.61)\n- Ensemble fusion: no significant improvement over DIF standalone\n- RL robustness: alignment-RL confirmed, reasoning-RL underpowered (N=13)\n- Bifactor IRT pilot: partial convergence (32/57), below CFI target\n- Item generation: difficulty calibration below target (ρ=0.58 vs 0.75)\n- Cumulative Bayesian DIF: incremental power gains\n- Graceful degradation: quantitative bounds on replacement\n- Item-Focused Trees: robustness to cohort definition (Jaccard=0.74 with MH)\n\n**References** (8 total in main paper):\n- Hendrycks et al. 2021 (MMLU)\n- Holland & Thayer 1988 (MH-DIF)\n- Mantel & Haenszel 1959 (original MH)\n- ETS 1999 (ETS standards)\n- Li et al. 2024 (task contamination)\n- Sheikhan et al. 2024 (ConStat)\n- Shi et al. 2024 (Min-K%)\n- Sinharay et al. 2006 (Bayesian DIF)\n- Tutz & Berger 2016 (Item-Focused Trees)\n- Sheikhan & Mukherjee 2024 (GSM1K)\n\n## Your Task\n\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Novelty assessment**: How novel is applying MH-DIF to LLM benchmarks? Is it a \"first application of known method to new domain\" or deeper? Is the cross-subject matching insight genuinely new?\n2. **Related work completeness**: Are important references missing? Is the positioning fair and accurate?\n3. **Contribution significance**: Would this paper change how the community thinks about benchmark evaluation? Is the diagnostic lens framing compelling?\n4. **Positioning and framing**: Is the paper positioned correctly relative to contamination detection literature? Is the \"instability ≠ invalidity\" distinction well-argued?\n5. **Audience and venue fit**: Is ICLR the right venue? Would reviewers at ICLR find this interesting?\n\nSpecifically consider these related work gaps:\n- Is there prior work on DIF in NLP/LLM settings that's missing?\n- Are there other psychometric approaches to benchmark validity not cited?\n- Is the contamination detection literature adequately covered (beyond ConStat, Min-K%, DVD)?\n- Are there benchmark dynamics papers (saturation, temporal validity) that should be cited?\n- \"Growing Pains\" is mentioned but not fully cited — identify this paper.\n\nProvide scores for: Overall (1-10), Soundness (1-4), Presentation (1-4), Contribution (1-4), Confidence (1-5).\n\nWrite your review in a structured format with: Summary, Strengths, Weaknesses, Questions for Authors, Missing References, Scores. Be thorough and critical but fair.\n","uuid":"92d327a3-5602-4b21-8710-a2bf1502a471","session_id":"583db7b7-de08-4fba-84bb-24a29bad8915"}
