{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01VTcaz5sa53cDn5h4BkH5ed","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01R8jG9eMooiREMqjsTCmnDs","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"# Dream Review Report — Full Roundtable\n\n**Paper**: \"Temporal Differential Item Functioning Reveals 31% Measurement Instability in MMLU: A Psychometric Audit of 5,227 Language Models\"\n\n**Review Mode**: Full (2 Reviewers, 4-step roundtable)\n**Target Venue**: ICLR (1-10 scale)\n**Date**: 2026-05-21\n\n---\n\n## Roundtable Summary\n\n| Criterion | R1 (Methods+Experiments) | R2 (Novelty+Related Work) | Consensus |\n|-----------|--------------------------|---------------------------|-----------|\n| Overall | 6 | 6 | **6** |\n| Soundness | 3/4 | 3/4 | **3/4** |\n| Presentation | 3/4 | 3/4 | **3/4** |\n| Contribution | 3/4 | 2/4 | **2.5/4** |\n| Confidence | 4/5 | 4/5 | 4/5 |\n\n**Consensus verdict**: Marginally above acceptance threshold. Genuine contributions in ground truth meta-critique and cross-subject matching methodology, but technical contribution is thin (unmodified classical method applied to new domain) and the flagship 31% finding lacks mechanistic decomposition. Honest negative result reporting and careful framing elevate the paper above the typical borderline submission. Likely needs a champion AC.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Paper Summary\n\nThe paper applies Mantel-Haenszel Differential Item Functioning (MH-DIF) — a classical psychometric method standardized since 1988 in educational testing — to the METABENCH MMLU response matrix (5,227 models × 12,508 items). It finds that 31.3% of MMLU items exhibit large temporal DIF (ETS Category C, |Δ_MH| ≥ 1.5) between 2023 and 2024-H1 model cohorts. This instability is not a statistical artifact (random-split sanity: 0.18%), not driven by ability differences (ability cohort: 2.9%), and predominantly item-specific (domain improvement explains ≤7.6%). The paper argues web-overlap contamination labels fundamentally cannot validate item-level differential detectors (enrichment ≈ 1.0) and provides a semi-synthetic injection framework with cross-subject matching that eliminates false-positive inflation (ΔFPR = 0.000). DIF items carry real cross-cohort signal but are the *least* disruptive non-random removal strategy, positioning DIF as a diagnostic lens rather than a contamination detector.\n\nFour claims: (1) temporal instability characterization, (2) ground truth meta-critique, (3) semi-synthetic validation framework, (4) DIF as diagnostic lens.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Strengths (Consensus)\n\n**S1. Ground truth meta-critique is the paper's most original intellectual contribution.** [R1+R2 strong agreement] The observation that web-overlap labels measure global exposure — not differential contamination — is simple once stated but not previously articulated with data. Enrichment ≈ 0.96 against Li et al. labels, combined with semi-synthetic ΔTPR = +0.508 at p = 0.5, provides a compelling two-part demonstration. This insight has implications for every paper validating item-level contamination detectors (Min-K%, ConStat, n-gram methods) against web-overlap ground truth. The field has routinely used these labels without recognizing the mismatch between \"publicly accessible\" and \"differentially functioning.\"\n\n**S2. Cross-subject matching is a genuine, reusable methodological contribution.** [R1+R2 strong agreement] The insight that within-subject matching contaminates the MH conditioning variable (FPR inflation +0.186) while cross-subject matching eliminates it (ΔFPR = 0.000 across all injection rates and all tested subjects) is non-obvious and practically useful. The dose-response curve (R² = 0.97) is convincing. GSM8K replication confirms portability. This contribution generalizes to any multi-domain benchmark where cross-domain scores can serve as matching variables.\n\n**S3. Unprecedented scale with rigorous sanity checks.** [R1 primary, R2 concurs] The 5,227 × 12,508 response matrix is the largest DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks. The random-split sanity check (C% = 0.18%) is exemplary. The ability-cohort control (C% = 2.9%), architecture check (99.98% decoder-only, Jaccard = 1.0), and threshold sensitivity analysis (C% robust at 10-49% across thresholds) collectively address the most obvious reviewer concerns preemptively.\n\n**S4. Honest treatment of negative results is exceptional.** [R1+R2 strong agreement] The paper transparently reports: DVD failure on MCQ (AUROC = 0.61, Appendix B), ensemble fusion non-significance (improvement CI includes zero, Appendix C), reasoning-RL underpowered (N = 13, Appendix D), bifactor IRT partial convergence (32/57 subjects, Appendix F), item generation below target (ρ = 0.58 vs 0.75 target, Appendix G), and GSM1K correlation attenuation under controls (r = −0.478 → −0.289). This level of transparency substantially exceeds the venue average and builds credibility.\n\n**S5. Circularity ablation effectively addresses the tautology concern.** [R1 primary] Comparing DIF removal (z = −9.5) against accuracy-gap (z = −157), high-variance (z = −108), and high-discrimination (z = −16) removal — each removing 31% of items — demonstrates that MH's ability conditioning genuinely isolates differential functioning. Despite 62.6% item overlap with accuracy-gap selection, DIF is 10-16× less disruptive. DIF-discrimination Jaccard = 0.12 confirms DIF captures something distinct from item quality.\n\n**S6. \"Instability ≠ invalidity\" framing is well-argued.** [R2 primary, R1 concurs] The combination of ρ ≈ 0.997 ranking stability after removing all DIF-C items with z = −8 to −49σ discriminative signal creates a nuanced picture. DIF items matter for understanding model differences but do not threaten aggregate validity. This framing preempts the most damaging misinterpretation.\n\n**S7. SIBTEST concordance and non-uniform DIF acknowledgment.** [R1+R2] The paper proactively addresses that MH detects only uniform DIF, reports SIBTEST identifies 11.2% additional non-uniform DIF items (total instability ~42.5%), and presents MH-SIBTEST Jaccard = 0.71 as evidence that uniform DIF captures the dominant signal. This honest reporting of a methodological limitation is commendable.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Weaknesses (Consensus)\n\n### Major\n\n**W1. The 31% \"instability\" conflates multiple distinct phenomena without quantitative decomposition.** [R1+R2 unanimous — the most significant weakness] The temporal DIF rate captures a mixture of: (a) genuine measurement instability, (b) differential capability improvement across item types, (c) training data exposure effects, (d) prompt sensitivity, and (e) model architecture/training recipe evolution. The domain-accuracy regression explains only 7.6% (plan_019), leaving 92.4% of the signal unexplained. The paper does not provide a taxonomy of *what kinds* of items exhibit DIF (factual recall vs. reasoning, item difficulty decile, question format). A reviewer asking \"31% of items changed — but changed in what way and why?\" receives no structured answer. This limits the diagnostic utility that the paper claims as its primary contribution.\n\n**W2. GSM1K validation (N = 33) is underpowered and over-featured.** [R1+R2 unanimous] The raw correlation r = −0.478 (p = 0.005) attenuates to r = −0.289 (p = 0.108) after controlling for accuracy and item difficulty, with BCa CI [−0.551, 0.012] crossing zero. Power to detect r = 0.30 with N = 33 and 3 controls is only 0.38. The paper appropriately downgrades to \"suggestive evidence\" in the text, but this result still appears in the abstract (\"does not threaten ranking validity\") and features prominently in Claim 4. With Spearman lower CI bound at −0.031, the evidence cannot strongly support \"DIF captures genuine capability change.\" This result should be presented even more cautiously and ideally removed from the abstract.\n\n**W3. Model non-independence violates MH assumptions.** [R2 primary, R1 concurs] The 5,227 models are not independent examinees — many share base weights (hundreds of Llama-2 fine-tunes, Mistral merges, etc.). This non-independence violates MH's assumption of independent observations within ability strata, potentially inflating chi-squared statistics and DIF flag rates. No sensitivity analysis restricting to base models only (or one model per family) is provided. This is a fundamental methodological concern that could affect the headline 31% figure.\n\n**W4. The technical contribution is thin: unmodified classical method applied to new domain.** [R2 primary] MH-DIF has been applied in educational testing for 38 years. The paper applies it without modification. The finding that \"items behave differently across groups\" is precisely what DIF detects, so the 31% result, while striking in magnitude, is not conceptually surprising. The interpretive contributions (ground truth meta-critique, diagnostic lens framing) elevate the paper, but at a methods-oriented venue like ICLR, the technical novelty ceiling is limited. The paper would benefit from discussing what is uniquely challenging about applying DIF to LLM populations versus human populations (extreme N, 57-dimensional ability space, non-independent examinees, rapidly evolving population).\n\n**W5. Uniform DIF only — non-uniform DIF inadequately addressed.** [R1 primary, R2 concurs] MH detects only uniform DIF (constant direction across ability levels). Non-uniform DIF is highly plausible in LLM settings: high-ability models may exploit contaminated items differently than low-ability models. The choice of MH over logistic regression DIF (Swaminathan & Rogers 1990, which naturally handles non-uniform DIF) is never justified. SIBTEST concordance is reported but the full methodology is not documented. This is not a minor preference — it affects which items are flagged and how the instability signal is interpreted.\n\n### Moderate\n\n**W6. Temporal cohort definition introduces uncontrolled confounds.** [R1] Release date correlates with training data cutoff, architecture generation, training recipe evolution (RLHF/DPO prevalence), and scale distribution. The paper controls for architecture (trivially, 99.98% decoder-only) but not the other correlated variables. A model released in January 2024 trained on data through 2022 is classified as \"2024\" despite 2022-era training data. Sensitivity to the temporal boundary (e.g., sliding window analysis) is not provided.\n\n**W7. The baseline FPR of 31% limits practical utility.** [R1] Three structural improvement attempts (matched-ability, IRT theta-conditioning, bifactor pre-check) all failed to reduce temporal baseline DIF below 31%. The paper reframes this as \"genuine temporal DIF,\" but if ~31% of items show DIF between any two temporal cohorts regardless of contamination, DIF cannot distinguish contaminated from non-contaminated items in practice. The semi-synthetic framework correctly measures ΔTPR, but the paper should be more explicit that DIF is a characterization tool, not a screening tool, precisely because the baseline rate is so high.\n\n**W8. Within-family analysis conflates DIF with legitimate capability evolution.** [R1] Llama-2 vs. Llama-3 at 61.3% C and Mistral vs. Mixtral at 38.7% C — these are not contamination-matched comparisons. The high C% in within-family comparisons is entirely expected when models have genuinely different capability profiles. This analysis is presented as supporting temporal instability but may primarily demonstrate that DIF detects capability differences, which is the impact-vs-DIF confound the paper acknowledges.\n\n**W9. Semi-synthetic injection oversimplifies contamination.** [R1] The incorrect→correct flipping at uniform probability p is deterministic. Real contamination is heterogeneous: it depends on item difficulty, distractor quality, format memorization, and model architecture. No sensitivity analysis to alternative injection models (e.g., difficulty-correlated flipping) is provided. ΔTPR estimates may not transfer to realistic contamination patterns.\n\n**W10. Missing related work.** [R2] Key omissions include:\n- Rodriguez et al. (2021) \"Evaluation Examples Are Not Equally Informative\" — directly relevant IRT-based item-level analysis (ACL 2021)\n- Ethayarajh & Jurafsky (2020) \"Utility is in the Eye of the User\" — benchmark validity arguments aligned with paper's framing\n- DIF/measurement invariance work in NLP fairness contexts (Plank et al., 2014; Sedoc & Ungar, 2020)\n- Domingue et al. (2022/2024) on IRT for LLM evaluation with measurement invariance discussion\n- The original Mantel & Haenszel (1959) appears in paper text but may be missing from formal references\n\n---\n\n## 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Decomposition**: Can you provide a quantitative decomposition of the 31.3% DIF-C items into component causes? What accounts for the 92.4% unexplained by domain-level accuracy improvement?\n\n2. **Model non-independence**: How sensitive is C% to restricting to one model per base architecture? Does the MH chi-squared inflation from correlated fine-tunes materially affect the 31% figure?\n\n3. **Logistic regression DIF**: Why was MH chosen over logistic regression DIF, which naturally handles non-uniform DIF? What is the computational cost comparison at this scale?\n\n4. **Item characterization**: Can you break down DIF-C items by difficulty decile, discrimination, subject category, and question format? The diagnostic lens claim requires knowing *what kinds* of items change, not just *how many*.\n\n5. **Temporal boundary sensitivity**: Have you tested C% with sliding window cohort definitions (e.g., 6-month windows)? Is DIF a step function or gradual trend?\n\n6. **Injection model sensitivity**: What happens with difficulty-correlated injection (e.g., flipping only on items above median difficulty)? Does ΔTPR change substantially?\n\n7. **Cross-subject matching independence**: If MMLU subjects share substantial common variance (total score correlates 0.9986 with IRT θ), does cross-subject matching truly provide independence? Can you quantify construct independence?\n\n8. **Cohort size discrepancy**: The paper states N_R = 1,253, N_F = 656 in the main text. Are these the final numbers after filtering? Please clarify any discrepancies.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Experiment Coverage Assessment\n\nComparing the paper against the imagined experiment list (review_context.yaml):\n\n| Experiment | Status | Coverage Quality |\n|-----------|--------|-----------------|\n| plan_001: Real data DIF MMLU | ✅ Main body §4.1 | Complete — all key results reported |\n| plan_002: RL robustness | ⚠️ Appendix D | Alignment-RL confirmed; reasoning-RL severely underpowered (N=13) |\n| plan_003a: ConStat comparison | ✅ Appendix A | Adequate — Jaccard=0.23 reported |\n| plan_003b: DVD generation variance | ✅ Appendix B | Honest negative (AUROC=0.61) |\n| plan_003c: Ensemble fusion | ✅ Appendix C | Honest negative (CI includes zero) |\n| plan_004: GSM8K + GSM1K | ⚠️ §4.3 + §4.4 supp | Steps 1-2 clean; Step 3 (GSM1K) appropriately downgraded but still over-featured |\n| plan_005: Bifactor IRT | ✅ Appendix F | Honest partial failure (32/57 converge) |\n| plan_006: Item generation | ✅ Appendix G | Below target (ρ=0.58) — negative pilot |\n| plan_007: Bayesian DIF | ✅ Appendix H | Incremental simulation result |\n| plan_008: Graceful degradation | ✅ Appendix I | Clean quantitative bounds |\n| plan_009: Item-Focused Trees | ✅ Appendix J | Robustness check (Jaccard=0.74) |\n| plan_010: Architecture stratification | ✅ §4.1 | Trivially resolved (99.98% decoder-only) |\n| plan_011: Semi-synthetic injection | ✅ §4.3 | Strong — cleanest methodological contribution |\n\n**Coverage assessment**: 13/13 imagined experiments reported. 5 in main body, 8 in appendices. Negative results transparently documented. The paper covers the full experiment design with appropriate triage of results.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Scoring Rationale\n\n### Overall: 6 (Marginally above acceptance threshold)\n\nThe paper makes genuine contributions: the ground truth meta-critique (Claim 2) has implications beyond this paper, the cross-subject matching framework (Claim 3) is a reusable methodological tool, and the scale of the empirical analysis is unprecedented. Honest negative reporting and careful framing elevate it above average borderline submissions.\n\nHowever, four factors prevent a higher score: (1) the technical contribution is limited — an unmodified 38-year-old method applied to new data; (2) the flagship 31% finding is descriptive rather than mechanistic, with no decomposition of the instability signal; (3) model non-independence is a fundamental threat to the MH statistics that is not addressed; (4) the GSM1K validation is underpowered and the attenuation pattern under controls weakens Claim 4.\n\nThe paper would benefit from: (a) a sensitivity analysis restricting to independent base models, (b) a taxonomy of DIF items by characteristics (difficulty, format, subject), (c) non-uniform DIF analysis via logistic regression, (d) more cautious featuring of the GSM1K result. With these revisions, the paper could reach 7-8.\n\n### Soundness: 3/4 (Good)\n\nMH-DIF application is technically correct within its assumptions. Controls are well-designed. Statistical procedures are valid. Deductions for: model non-independence unaddressed, uniform-DIF-only limitation without justification for not using logistic regression DIF, IRT model misfit implications not quantified, and semi-synthetic injection model simplicity.\n\n### Presentation: 3/4 (Good)\n\nClear writing with the \"instability ≠ invalidity\" framing established early. Well-structured narrative arc from characterization through meta-critique to validation to interpretation. Honest treatment of negative results. 10 appendices (A-J) provide thorough supplementary material. Deductions for: content density exceeding typical ICLR length, some analyses (RL robustness, bifactor pilot) occupying disproportionate space relative to evidential weight, and the GSM1K result over-featured relative to its statistical support.\n\n### Contribution: 2.5/4 (Fair to Good)\n\nR1 and R2 disagreed here (3 vs 2). The compromise reflects: the ground truth meta-critique and cross-subject matching are genuine contributions to LLM evaluation methodology (supporting 3), but the core analysis applies an unmodified classical method without domain-specific adaptation, and the 31% finding is descriptive rather than mechanistic (supporting 2). The paper is stronger as an empirical characterization than as a methods paper, which creates tension at a methods-oriented venue.\n\n### Confidence: 4/5\n\nBoth reviewers are familiar with DIF/IRT methodology and LLM evaluation literature. The review draws on thorough reading of the full paper, all appendices, and the experiment design context.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Recommendations for Revision\n\n**Must-fix for acceptance**:\n1. Add sensitivity analysis restricting to independent base models (one per family) to address the model non-independence concern.\n2. Provide item-level DIF characterization: what types of items (by difficulty, format, domain cluster) are disproportionately flagged? This is essential for the \"diagnostic lens\" claim.\n3. Justify the choice of MH over logistic regression DIF, or add logistic regression DIF results.\n4. Remove or substantially reduce GSM1K correlation from the abstract and Claim 4 framing; present it only as supplementary exploratory evidence.\n\n**Should-fix**:\n5. Discuss what is uniquely challenging about applying DIF to LLM populations vs. human populations (non-independence, extreme N, rapidly evolving population, multidimensional ability).\n6. Add missing references (Rodriguez et al. 2021, Ethayarajh & Jurafsky 2020, Mantel & Haenszel 1959).\n7. Test sensitivity to temporal boundary (sliding window analysis).\n8. Test sensitivity to injection model (difficulty-correlated flipping).\n\n**Nice-to-have**:\n9. Consolidate appendices F-H into a shorter \"Future Directions\" section.\n10. Promote Item-Focused Trees (Appendix J) to a main-body robustness check — it directly validates that the DIF finding is not an artifact of arbitrary cohort boundaries.\n","project":"irt_equated_bench_regen","tier":"median","dream_id":"20260521T042647","overall_score":"borderline accept (6/10)","review_visible":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6328,"cache_read_input_tokens":70677,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6328},"output_tokens":49,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"583db7b7-de08-4fba-84bb-24a29bad8915","uuid":"7040e472-833c-4514-bd65-aae8e9b22efe"}
